WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.670
Today we're going to find things in a list.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:04.590
But there's a catch.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.310
Spotting the answer yourself isn't enough.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:10.070
You need to show how the computer finds it.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.830
Your WAGBA today is to get better at

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:14.760
following and explaining search algorithms,

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.230
so that we can choose the correct steps to find an item.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.510
For Knowledge, you'll recognise the target,

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.370
index, stored value and midpoint.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.610
You've learned about lists in the previous sessions.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.110
You'll also learn why binary search needs sorted data.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:32.090
For Skills, you'll follow the numbered pseudocode

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:35.510
and record each step on your paper booklet.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:36.730
The booklet should be given to you.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:39.850
For Understanding, you'll explain why the search moves,

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.330
changes its search area or stops.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.280
By the end, I want you to look at a search step

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:46.710
and confidently explain:

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:49.820
“This is what happens next—and this is why.”

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:51.470
You don't need to rush.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:55.130
We're practising careful thinking, not fast clicking.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:59.030
First of all, what does a linear or binary search look like?

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.810
Let's try finding book number 31.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.910
Our list contains five, nine, thirteen,

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.950
eighteen, twenty-four and thirty-one.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.110
A linear search starts at the beginning.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.510
We compare the value and ask ourselves:

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:17.690
“Is that 31?” No.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.350
Next, 9? No match.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.190
It keeps moving through the list until it reaches 31.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.090
In this example, it checks six items.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.850
Now, binary search begins at the midpoint,

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.370
index 3, which stores 18.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:36.350
31 is greater than 18.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:38.770
Because the list is sorted,

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.850
we can rule out 18 and everything before it.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:43.470
The next midpoint stores 31.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:45.950
Found—in two item checks.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.550
Notice that index 3 and value 18 are different things.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.630
Keep those separate in your explanation.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:57.010
You'll study these supplied examples first.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.600
Then you'll trace a different target yourself

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:00.630
and explain your decisions.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.250
Where can you learn more about linear or binary search?

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.010
Here's the difference to remember.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:09.310
Linear search checks items in sequence.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.430
The data doesn't need to be sorted.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.910
However, binary search needs sorted data

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.730
to check the midpoint

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.530
and narrow down the possible search area.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:24.980
In Main Activity 1 and Main Activity 2 today,

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:27.230
you'll find readings and worked examples,

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:31.010
and Craig ’n’ Dave videos I've recommended.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:33.970
Please watch them fully.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:36.700
You may pause, rewind and use the step controls

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.190
whenever something needs another look.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:43.370
Open Teams and choose Year 10 Computer Science.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.110
Go to Week 10 Session 2—Linear and Binary Search.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.590
Select “01. Start here”.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:58.290
Enter your name and class in the website.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:01.090
Begin with the starter, one question at a time.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.620
When you finish, add photographs of your work

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:08.260
and export it as a PDF.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:10.060
Attach your evidence and submit it in Teams.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:12.870
That's your starting point. Good luck, everybody.
